Greedy Decoding

Choose the most likely token at each step

for t in range(max_length):
    next_token = argmax(P(y_t | y_{&lt;t}, x))
    output.append(next_token)
    if next_token == end_of_sequence_token:
        break
# Pytorch
for _ in range(max_length):
    outputs = model(input_ids) # run forward pass
    logits = outputs.logits[0, -1, :] # chose last token's logits
    next_token = torch.argmax(logits).unsqueeze(0).unsqueeze(0) # chose logit with max prob {.fragment highlight-current-red}
    input_ids = torch.cat([input_ids, next_token], dim=1) # append input tokn back to prompt

Computing attention weights for all tokens

Compute attention scores for all tokens

attn_scores = torch.zeros(inputs.shape[0], inputs.shape[0])

for i, x_i in enumerate(inputs):
    for j, x_j in enumerate(inputs):
        attn_scores[i, j] = torch.dot(x_i, x_j)

Normalize scores to get attention weights

attn_weights = torch.softmax(attn_scores, dim=-1)

Compute output/context vectors for all tokens

output_vectors = torch.zeros_like(inputs)
for i in range(inputs.shape[0]):
    for j in range(inputs.shape[0]):
        output_vectors[i] += attn_weights[i, j] * inputs[j]

Multi-Head Attention : Naive Implementation

# naive version using loops

class MultiHeadAttentionWrapper(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, d_in, d_out, context_length, dropout, num_heads, qkv_bias=False):
        super().__init__()
        self.heads = nn.ModuleList([
            CausalAttention(d_in, d_out, context_length, dropout, qkv_bias)
            for _ in range(num_heads)
        ])

    def forward(self, x):
        return torch.cat([head(x) for head in self.heads], dim=-1)

Relative Position and Rotation
Moving \(k\) steps forward is exactly a rotation by \(\omega k\):




\[
\begin{pmatrix}\sin(\omega(p{+}k))\\ \cos(\omega(p{+}k))\end{pmatrix}
=
\underbrace{\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\omega k) & \sin(\omega k)\\ -\sin(\omega k) & \cos(\omega k)\end{pmatrix}}_{\text{rotation by }\omega k}
\begin{pmatrix}\sin(\omega p)\\ \cos(\omega p)\end{pmatrix}
\]

The rotation depends only on the shift \(k\), never on the absolute position \(p\).
So relative distance is, in principle, recoverable from the absolute encoding.

Chat Templates
Many models are fine-tuned as chatbots with messages format:
messages = [
    {
        "role": "system",
        "content": "Please classify movie reviews as 'positive' or 'negative'."
    },
    {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "This movie is a banger."
    },
]
Roles:

System: Sets behavior and context for the conversation
User: Provides input or asks questions
Assistant: Model’s responses

Chat Format: Llama 3 Example
Messages are converted to strings with special tags:
messages = [
    {
        "role": "system",
        "content": "You are a helpful assistant that helps a student understand complex topics"
    },
    {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "Can you explain how photosynthesis works?"
    }
]

input_text = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False)
print(input_text)

# Output:
&lt;|begin_of_text|&gt;&lt;|start_header_id|&gt;system&lt;|end_header_id|&gt;
You are a helpful assistant that helps a student understand complex topics
&lt;|start_header_id|&gt;user&lt;|end_header_id|&gt;
Can you explain how photosynthesis works?
Every model family has a different template. Chosing the wrong template for a model produces garbage outputs:

Mistral: [INST] ... [/INST]
Qwen: &lt;|im_start|&gt;system\n...&lt;|im_end|&gt;\n&lt;|im_start|&gt;user\n...&lt;|im_end|&gt;
Gemma: &lt;start_of_turn&gt;user\n...&lt;end_of_turn&gt;\n&lt;start_of_turn&gt;model

